Targets: wasm
Releases: 11.0-11.17
Subject: Give ExecShutdownNode's tree walker the two arguments it is called with
Purpose: planstate_tree_walker() takes its walker as a pointer to a function declared without a prototype and calls it with two arguments (the plan state and the context). ExecShutdownNode(), which 9.6 passes to it, has one parameter. Natively the extra argument is harmless; in WebAssembly an indirect call whose number of arguments does not match the function's type traps ("null function or function signature mismatch"), and the first query that runs a plan, in initdb's post-bootstrap phase, stops there. The walker is a small function with the two parameters that calls ExecShutdownNode(). This variant is for 10.0 to 10.22, which call planstate_tree_walker() from the start of ExecShutdownNode() and ignore its result; 10.23 has a walker of its own with two parameters (upstream), so no patch is needed there. The sibling for 9.6 is capped at 9.6.24. This copy is for 11.0 to 11.17: 11.18 got the same fix from upstream (a walker with two parameters), as 10.23 did, and a patch has one range of releases.
Components: src/backend/executor/execProcnode.c
Checks: scripts/build-wasm.sh creates the template data directory (initdb runs queries through the executor); the browser tests run queries
Origin: own

diff --git a/src/backend/executor/execProcnode.c b/src/backend/executor/execProcnode.c
--- a/src/backend/executor/execProcnode.c
+++ b/src/backend/executor/execProcnode.c
@@ -719,2 +719,11 @@
 
+#ifdef __EMSCRIPTEN__
+/* planstate_tree_walker() calls its walker with (node, context); WebAssembly traps on an indirect call with the wrong number of arguments */
+static bool
+ExecShutdownNodeWalker(PlanState *node, void *context)
+{
+	return ExecShutdownNode(node);
+}
+#endif
+
 /*
@@ -737,3 +746,7 @@
 
+#ifdef __EMSCRIPTEN__
+	planstate_tree_walker(node, ExecShutdownNodeWalker, NULL);
+#else
 	planstate_tree_walker(node, ExecShutdownNode, NULL);
+#endif
 
